The eleven verbs that hang directly off the root rather than off a family.

`completions` is the only one here that answers without a bus; the rest need a
fleet to say anything, so what is pinned is their interface, which is exactly
the part a renderer refactor can break without noticing.

`doctor` is here for a second reason: its `--listen-for <SECS>` and the global
`-l, --listen <LISTEN>` are two arguments that used to be one name, and the
help below is where that would show again (#239).

```console
$ zenctl get --help
GET any selector with the fleet discipline (RFC 05 §2.1).

Target All, consolidation None, every reply attributed by its own key; error envelopes render as errors (RFC 05 §3), and payloads ride the same rendering ladder as `topic echo` (served-schema decode → structural → text → hex). `@/**` browses the zenoh admin space. Exit codes: 0 values only, 1 an error reply, 2 silence.

Usage: zenctl get [OPTIONS] <SELECTOR>

Arguments:
  <SELECTOR>
          Any key expression, params included (`key?k=v`)

Options:
      --body <BODY>
          Query body: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin — rides the same encode ladder as `topic pub` when the selector's key part refines to a registered subject

      --raw
          Ship the body verbatim and print payloads as hex; no decode

      --hex
          Decode the type name, print the payload as hex

      --fmt <TEMPLATE>
          Per-reply line template — the `topic echo` % vocabulary (%k %K %o %c %p %s %t %v %e %l %n %a %{a.b.c})

      --no-decode
          Skip schema decode; render structurally

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl serve --help
Stand up a mock queryable: answer every query on a keyexpr with one static body, and log every ask (#121).

The log doubles as a "who is querying this key" probe. Deliberately no reply scripting — static and file bodies cover the dev-loop case; the shell covers dynamic replies by restarting serve. (nuze and zsak own the embedded-language lane, at the cost of a Nushell dependency and a linked libpython respectively.)

Usage: zenctl serve [OPTIONS] <KEYEXPR> <REPLY>

Arguments:
  <KEYEXPR>
          Key expression to serve (full wire form; wildcards welcome)

  <REPLY>
          Reply body: inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin (read once) — through the same encode ladder as `topic pub`

Options:
      --encoding <ENCODING>
          Wire encoding to declare on replies. Defaults to the registry's declared encoding when the keyexpr refines, else none

      --no-validate
          Do not refuse a body the served schema rejects

      --raw
          Reply the bytes verbatim: no schema lookup, no refusal

      --complete
          Declare the queryable complete — a claim this responder holds ALL the data the expression names. Say it only when you mean it

      --count <COUNT>
          Exit after N queries (0 = until ctrl-c)
          
          [default: 0]

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl cutover --help
Cutover acceptance, half one (RFC 09 §6): assert a retired key family SILENT while the new plane carries traffic.

Three verdicts, three exits: 0 = old silent AND new speaking; 1 = the old family still speaks; 2 = everything was quiet — a non-verdict, because a dead fleet passes the silence half for free. The leak check's meaning is stated, not inferred: anything outside `<base>/v1/` that is not the old root.

Usage: zenctl cutover [OPTIONS] --old-root <KEYEXPR>

Options:
      --old-root <KEYEXPR>
          The retired key family (a full wire key expression)

      --window <WINDOW>
          Listening window, seconds
          
          [default: 30]

      --base <BASE>
