$ zenctl record --help
Capture a selector's traffic to a .zrec file (RFC 09 §5.2).

Through the Monitor: a bus that outruns the disk surfaces as drop records *in the file*, where the gaps happened (RFC 09 §5.1 O6) — a capture is a bounded observer and says what it cost. Replay with `zenctl replay`; the file is ndjson (one row per line, payloads lossless as base64 `bytes`), so `jq` reads it too.

Usage: zenctl record [OPTIONS] --out <FILE> [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          Full wire selector to capture. Defaults to all v1 data under the base: `<base>/v1/**` — which `**` being unable to cross `@`-chunks makes media-safe, and blind to the verbatim planes

Options:
      --origin <ORIGIN>
          Only this origin (`h-…` or `@service`)

      --class <CLASS>
          Only this class: telemetry, state, or events

      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer

  -o, --out <FILE>
          Output file

      --duration <SECS>
          Stop after this many seconds

      --count <COUNT>
          Stop after this many samples (0 = until ctrl-c or --duration)
          
          [default: 0]

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

