$ zenctl replay --help
Replay a .zrec capture onto the bus — replay is PUBLISHING.

Real puts through declared publishers, at the capture's own pacing (scaled by --speed), re-stamped with this session's HLC: re-stamped old data WINS last-writer-wins against a live fleet, which is why the etiquette is enforced (RFC 09 §5.2) — dry-run first, and the capture header's base is a contract (`--force-base` to override).

Usage: zenctl replay [OPTIONS] <FILE>

Arguments:
  <FILE>
          The .zrec file to replay

Options:
      --speed <SPEED>
          Pacing scale: 2.0 replays twice as fast as captured
          
          [default: 1]

      --dry-run
          List every would-be put and publish nothing (no session is even opened). Preview pacing is not simulated

      --force-base
          Replay even though the resolved base differs from the capture header's

      --i-know
          Replay recorded deletes that fall off the state class — the same operator price as `topic retire` (RFC 04 §1.2, v1.12)

      --qos <QOS>
          QoS profile for rows that recorded none
          
          [default: refreshed]

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl scout --help
Listen for raw scouting Hellos: zid, whatami, locators.

The layer *below* `base list`: no session is opened, so this answers "is anything out there at all" and "is multicast working on this segment". It is also the one zenctl verb where multicast is ON by default — scouting is the point, and a scout only listens for Hellos and joins nothing.

Usage: zenctl scout [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --what <WHAT>
          Filter by advertised kind. Repeatable; default: all three
          
          [possible values: router, peer, client]

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to listen (default 5; a context may override)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable — reaches gossip scouting where multicast is filtered

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context for endpoints/timeout defaults

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl completions --help
Generate shell completions (bash, zsh, fish, elvish, powershell).

e.g. `zenctl completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/zenctl`

The default script is **dynamic** (issue #54): it calls back into `zenctl` so producer, subject, type and procedure names come from the cached registry of the active context. The cache is written by any command that loads slices; completion never touches the bus, so a `<TAB>` cannot hang on a fleet that is down. `--static` emits the old self-contained script instead.

Usage: zenctl completions [OPTIONS] <SHELL>

Arguments:
  <SHELL>
          [possible values: bash, elvish, fish, powershell, zsh]

Options:
      --static
          Emit the self-contained script: no callbacks, no cached names

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl doctor --help
Diff what the fleet *serves* against local registry files.

RFC 08 §6: "A disagreement between introspection and the checked-in TOML is a finding, not an ambiguity." This prints the findings. The local truth comes from `--registry <dir>`; without it only the roster-vs-introspect check runs.

Usage: zenctl doctor [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --deep
          Additionally GET current state to check freshness against each subject's ttl (RFC 04 §1.2) and judge storage coverage — adds fleet query load

      --sample <N>
          With --deep: drain at most N state samples per family — bounds the sweep's cost, not just its output

      --listen-for <SECS>
          Listen passively to the data planes for this many seconds after the GET fan-in and judge what rides (#161): undecodable/invalid payloads, declared-vs-observed QoS, unregistered traffic, over-rate events. The report states the window, its scopes, and what the bounded observer dropped (O5/O6)

      --fail-on <SEVERITY>
          Exit 1 when a finding at (or above) this severity exists. Default: always exit 0 — findings are output, not verdicts

          Possible values:
          - error:   Fail on error-severity findings only
          - warning: Fail on warnings or errors

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
